2)  Who will do Unit Testing and What is the importance of Unit Testing? Explain about Quality Control :

       Developer :  The developer who writes the code is responsible for unit testing.
                    They know the logic best and test each function/method they write before committing.

    What is unit testing?
     Unit testing means testing the smallest individual piece of code — one function,
    one method, or one class — in complete isolation from the rest of the application.
    The goal is to verify that each unit does exactly what it is supposed to do.


     Importance of unit testing :

1) Catches bugs early — at the code level
Finding a bug in unit testing costs 10x less than finding it in production. Fix now, not later.

2) Makes code reliable and predictable
Each unit is verified to work correctly. Other developers can trust and reuse that code confidently.

3) Supports safe refactoring
When you change code later, unit tests instantly tell you if you broke anything. No guessing needed.

4) Acts as living documentation
Unit tests show exactly how a function is supposed to behave — new developers read tests to understand code.

5) Speeds up debugging
When a test fails, you know exactly which unit broke. No need to search through 10,000 lines of code.

6) Enables CI/CD pipelines
Automated unit tests run on every code push. If tests fail, the build stops. This is the foundation of DevOps.

7) Reduces overall testing cost
Fewer bugs reach QA, UAT, and production. Less rework saves money, time, and reputation.

Quality Control :

QC is the activity of testing the actual product to find and fix defects before release.
It is done by the QA team after development is complete.






 
